Scott asks, for effectively given domains \(D_0=\{X_n\}\), \(D_1=\{Y_m\}\), and \(D_2=\{Z_k\}\), to finish the curry case of Theorem 7.5 by writing curry as a relation between neighbourhoods and to decide whether that set of pairs is recursive or only recursively enumerable. `curryComb_rel` states the relation: a neighbourhood \(G\) of \(((D_0\times D_1)\to D_2)\) is related to a neighbourhood \(H\) of \((D_0\to(D_1\to D_2))\) exactly when every approximable map in \(G\) has its curry in \(H\). One direction rewrites the image filter by `curryIso_apply`, takes the least map of the principal neighbourhood of \(G\), and uses monotonicity of curry together with upward closure of the function space; the converse uses `toApproxMap_principal_mem` to put that least map back into \(G\). On the function-space presentations of Theorem 7.5, `curryComb_rel_recDecidable` turns the test into a bounded double universal quantifier over coded entries: the outer guard holds only if every inner entry that is itself a neighbourhood has each of its steps contained in the source neighbourhood after reindexing by `curryStepCode`. That code is the successor of a pairing that builds the one-entry step from a product index and an inner step, and `primrec_curryStepCode` shows the reindexing is primitive recursive, so inclusion after it is decided by composing the presentation's inclusion decider with the reindexing. Bounded universal quantification and the rewriting of an implication as a disjunction against a negated guard keep the whole predicate decidable. `curryComb_isComputable` passes from that decidable predicate to a recursively enumerable one and concludes that curry is a computable map. `example_716` is the conjunction of the relation, its decidability, and that computability theorem. **Theorem:** yes — curry's neighbourhood relation is written out and is recursively decidable, hence recursive. **Proof:** yes — the least-map reduction, the curry-step reindexing, and the bounded inclusion checks are the neighbourhood calculation Theorem 7.5 left open.
